1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,240
Cut a gold coin in half. Then cut it
again. And again. How small can you go

2
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:12,400
before it stops being gold? What's really
inside an atom? By the end of this film

3
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:17,660
you'll know why the periodic table is in
the order it is, and how we proved it.

4
00:00:17,980 --> 00:00:24,760
Because in 1869 the table had a secret
problem. It was sorted by weight, and some

5
00:00:24,760 --> 00:00:30,980
elements refused to fit. If weight was the
rule, the rule was broken, and nobody knew

6
00:00:30,980 --> 00:00:37,620
why. For two thousand years the atom was
just an idea: the smallest piece, the one

7
00:00:37,620 --> 00:00:44,640
you can't cut. Nobody had ever seen one.
Then, in a glowing glass tube, someone

8
00:00:44,640 --> 00:00:50,260
found a piece smaller than the atom. Then
a tiny bullet bounced back off a sheet of

9
00:00:50,260 --> 00:00:55,640
gold, as if it had hit a wall. And the
final proof came from a young physicist

10
00:00:55,640 --> 00:01:02,720
with a beam of X-rays, who counted what
was inside every element. Four people, one

11
00:01:02,720 --> 00:01:08,620
century, and a question that started with
a knife and a coin. So why was the table

12
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:09,640
in the wrong order?

13
00:01:10,533 --> 00:01:15,033
Manchester, eighteen-oh-eight. A
schoolteacher named John Dalton puts the

14
00:01:15,033 --> 00:01:19,673
old idea to work. His atomic theory
says every element is made of its own kind

15
00:01:19,673 --> 00:01:25,593
of atom, and each kind has its own weight.
Hydrogen is the lightest. Oxygen is

16
00:01:25,593 --> 00:01:31,213
heavier. Gold is heavier still. Why
believe him? Because elements always

17
00:01:31,213 --> 00:01:36,253
combine in the same fixed amounts. Water
is always the same mix of hydrogen and

18
00:01:36,253 --> 00:01:40,693
oxygen, however you make it. That only
makes sense if both are made of tiny

19
00:01:40,693 --> 00:01:46,073
pieces with fixed weights. Now chemists
had a ruler. Compare the weights of the

20
00:01:46,073 --> 00:01:51,813
atoms and line them up. Sixty years later
in Saint Petersburg, Dmitri Mendeleev does

21
00:01:51,813 --> 00:01:58,013
exactly that. He writes each element on a
card and sorts the cards by weight. The

22
00:01:58,013 --> 00:02:03,393
properties repeat. Soft metals that fizz
in water line up in one column. Harsh,

23
00:02:03,573 --> 00:02:09,593
reactive chlorine, bromine and iodine line
up in another. He even leaves gaps for

24
00:02:09,593 --> 00:02:14,753
elements nobody has found yet. Then he
prints his table and mails it to chemists

25
00:02:14,753 --> 00:02:20,653
across Europe. But look closer. Tellurium
is heavier than iodine, so by weight

26
00:02:20,653 --> 00:02:26,113
tellurium should come after it. Mendeleev
puts it before anyway, because that's

27
00:02:26,113 --> 00:02:31,393
where it behaves right. Cobalt and nickel
have the same trouble. Mendeleev guessed

28
00:02:31,393 --> 00:02:37,093
the weights had been measured wrong. They
hadn't. So the table works, but it breaks

29
00:02:37,093 --> 00:02:41,393
its own rule. If weight isn't the real
order, what is?

30
00:02:42,411 --> 00:02:48,071
Cambridge, eighteen ninety-seven. J.J.
Thomson is studying a strange glow. Pump

31
00:02:48,071 --> 00:02:52,231
the air out of a glass tube, run
electricity through it, and a beam shoots

32
00:02:52,231 --> 00:02:56,991
from one end to the other. Scientists
called them cathode rays. But what were

33
00:02:56,991 --> 00:03:04,271
they made of? Light, or stuff? Thomson
brings a magnet close. The beam bends. He

34
00:03:04,271 --> 00:03:10,771
tries two charged metal plates. It bends
again. Light doesn't do that. Tiny charged

35
00:03:10,771 --> 00:03:15,991
particles do. Then he measures how far
they bend, and works out how heavy they

36
00:03:15,991 --> 00:03:22,071
must be. The answer is astonishing: far
lighter than a hydrogen atom, the lightest

37
00:03:22,071 --> 00:03:28,251
thing anyone knew. We now know each one is
about two thousand times lighter. But here

38
00:03:28,251 --> 00:03:33,231
is the strange part. It doesn't matter
which metal he uses. The same particles

39
00:03:33,231 --> 00:03:39,891
come out every time. So the uncuttable
thing can be cut. Every atom carries these

40
00:03:39,891 --> 00:03:45,911
tiny negative particles. Thomson called
them corpuscles. Today we call them

41
00:03:45,911 --> 00:03:51,391
electrons. But atoms have no overall
charge, so something positive must balance

42
00:03:51,391 --> 00:03:56,431
them out. A few years later, in
nineteen-oh-four, Thomson made his guess:

43
00:03:57,051 --> 00:04:01,931
a soft ball of positive stuff with
electrons dotted through it, like raisins

44
00:04:01,931 --> 00:04:06,111
in a pudding. It was a lovely idea. It was
also wrong.

45
00:04:06,844 --> 00:04:12,264
Manchester, nineteen-oh-nine. Ernest
Rutherford has a new kind of bullet: alpha

46
00:04:12,264 --> 00:04:18,604
particles, tiny and fast, thrown out by
radioactive rocks. His assistant Hans

47
00:04:18,604 --> 00:04:23,964
Geiger and a young student called Ernest
Marsden fire them at gold beaten thinner

48
00:04:23,964 --> 00:04:28,964
than paper. Picture yourself in that room.
The lights are off, and you sit in the

49
00:04:28,964 --> 00:04:33,624
dark until your eyes adjust. You look
through a microscope at a small screen.

50
00:04:34,364 --> 00:04:40,364
Each time a particle hits it, you see a
tiny green flash, and you count it. Now,

51
00:04:40,704 --> 00:04:44,944
if Thomson's pudding is right, every
bullet should punch straight through.

52
00:04:45,564 --> 00:04:51,164
Almost all of them do. But about one in
eight thousand bounces back. Rutherford

53
00:04:51,164 --> 00:04:56,044
said it was as if you fired a huge shell
at a piece of tissue paper, and it came

54
00:04:56,044 --> 00:05:01,264
back and hit you. Then, in
nineteen-eleven, he explains it. Nearly

55
00:05:01,264 --> 00:05:06,904
all of an atom's mass is packed into a
tiny, positive centre, which he calls the

56
00:05:06,904 --> 00:05:13,084
nucleus. The electrons sit far outside
it. Everything in between is almost empty.

57
00:05:13,724 --> 00:05:18,464
How empty? Imagine you are standing in the
middle of a football stadium, holding a

58
00:05:18,464 --> 00:05:23,944
pea. If the atom were the whole stadium,
the nucleus would be that pea. The

59
00:05:23,944 --> 00:05:29,144
electrons would be somewhere out past the
back row of seats. Everything else, all

60
00:05:29,144 --> 00:05:35,244
around you, is nothing at all. That raised
a new question. If every element has its

61
00:05:35,244 --> 00:05:40,884
own nucleus, what makes one nucleus
different from another? A Dutch lawyer and

62
00:05:40,884 --> 00:05:47,084
amateur scientist, Antonius van den Broek,
had a bold guess: an element's place in

63
00:05:47,084 --> 00:05:52,844
the table might equal the positive charge
in its nucleus. It was a brilliant guess,

64
00:05:53,264 --> 00:05:55,624
but nobody could measure it. Yet.

65
00:05:56,578 --> 00:06:02,098
Manchester, nineteen thirteen. Henry
Moseley is twenty-five. He builds a tube

66
00:06:02,098 --> 00:06:06,338
with a little train inside, carrying a
different metal target into the beam each

67
00:06:06,338 --> 00:06:12,358
time. He fires electrons at each target,
and each metal gives off X-rays. It's the

68
00:06:12,358 --> 00:06:17,558
same invisible light Röntgen used to see
the bones in a living hand. Moseley splits

69
00:06:17,558 --> 00:06:22,278
the X-rays with a crystal and measures
their wavelength. Then he finds a pattern.

70
00:06:23,098 --> 00:06:28,338
Element by element, the X-rays step up in
perfectly even jumps, like the rungs of a

71
00:06:28,338 --> 00:06:34,238
ladder. One rung per element, no matter
how heavy it is. Count the rungs and you

72
00:06:34,238 --> 00:06:39,818
get a whole number for every element: the
positive charge in its nucleus. Van den

73
00:06:39,818 --> 00:06:45,118
Broek's guess was right. Now sort the
table by that number. Tellurium is

74
00:06:45,118 --> 00:06:50,998
fifty-two and iodine is fifty-three.
Cobalt is twenty-seven and nickel is

75
00:06:50,998 --> 00:06:57,078
twenty-eight. The broken rule fixes
itself. Mendeleev's instinct was right all

76
00:06:57,078 --> 00:07:02,438
along. He was just using the wrong ruler.
And the ladder had missing rungs, at

77
00:07:02,438 --> 00:07:08,558
forty-three, sixty-one, seventy-two and
seventy-five. Those were elements nobody

78
00:07:08,558 --> 00:07:14,018
had found yet. Over the next thirty-odd
years, every one of them was found in

79
00:07:14,018 --> 00:07:19,658
nature or made in a lab. Why do the
columns repeat? The charge in the nucleus

80
00:07:19,658 --> 00:07:24,638
decides how many electrons an atom holds,
and the outer electrons decide how it

81
00:07:24,638 --> 00:07:30,138
behaves. Elements with the same kind of
outer electrons end up in the same column.

82
00:07:30,758 --> 00:07:36,018
That's why the soft metals fizz together,
and why chlorine and iodine react alike.

83
00:07:36,758 --> 00:07:41,478
Two years later, in nineteen fifteen,
Moseley was killed in the First World War

84
00:07:41,478 --> 00:07:46,378
at Gallipoli. He was twenty-seven. Many
scientists believe he would have won a

85
00:07:46,378 --> 00:07:52,598
Nobel Prize. So this is what's really
inside an atom. First it was an idea. Then

86
00:07:52,598 --> 00:07:59,138
Dalton gave each kind a weight. Thomson
found a piece inside it. Rutherford found

87
00:08:01,558 --> 00:08:05,638
its tiny centre. And Moseley counted it.
Every element has its own number, and that

88
00:08:05,638 --> 00:08:10,818
number is the order of the periodic table.
It isn't a list of weights. It's a count.

89
00:08:11,558 --> 00:08:17,498
But the nucleus had one more secret. Some
nuclei don't sit still. They break apart

90
00:08:17,498 --> 00:08:20,644
and throw out rays of their own. That's
the next story.
